.TH QUADRF-ETHERNET 8 "August 2026" "QuadRF" "QuadRF"
.SH NAME
quadrf-ethernet \- DHCP client or direct-connect island on eth0
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B quadrf-ethernet
.RI [ start | stop | watch ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Follows carrier on eth0. On link up it asks for a DHCP lease. If a
router answers, the appliance stays a LAN client and publishes
.B quadrf.local
on that interface. No DHCP server is started, so a home router keeps
owning the subnet.
.PP
If nothing answers, eth0 becomes a direct-connect island at
.B 10.55.1.1/24
with dnsmasq handing
.B 10.55.1.2
through
.B 10.55.1.10
to the attached machine. There is no default route, so an unplugged
cable cannot take over outbound traffic.
.PP
A leftover lease is not treated as a LAN. The default gateway and DHCP
server from the lease must still answer on this link (ICMP or ARP);
otherwise the address is dropped and the DHCP-or-direct probe runs
again. That is what happens when the cable moves from a router to a PC:
ifupdown does not see carrier loss on the onboard NIC as an ifdown.
.PP
.B quadrf-ethernet.service
runs
.B watch
and re-evaluates on carrier change.
.B start
configures once, or signals the watcher if it is already running. The
ifupdown stanza in
.I /etc/network/interfaces.d/quadrf-eth0.cfg
calls
.B start
when the device appears.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/dnsmasq.d/quadrf-eth0-direct.conf
DHCP range and names, only while direct-connect is up.
.TP
.I /etc/dnsmasq.d/quadrf-eth0-lan.conf
Names only, while eth0 holds a router lease.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR quadrf-hotspot (8),
.BR quadrf (1)
